Well, it is Lojban terminology at work again: mi and do are sets, if anythi=
ng with a name (L-sets), so "union" seems appropriate, while "along with" s=
ounds like accompanying, not union.=A0 {lo}expressions are also sets in the=
 same sense.=A0 If {lo'i} means something else, it means C-sets, which are =
virtually useless for linguistic purposes and belong, if at all, to MEX.=A0=
=20
In passing, I note {me} being used for "is a member of", which is not what =
it means, although I suppose it does include that meaning as a remote part.




